"""The app-started job slice (`docs/design/agent-jobs.md`), driven through a
REAL subprocess exactly as `test_agent_api.py` drives the chat `ask()` flow --
`AgentSettings.executable` points at the same `fake_agent_cli.py`, which under
`--input-format stream-json` behaves as the real CLI measures: the prompt comes
on stdin, each frame is a turn, `--replay-user-messages` echoes it, and the
process exits once stdin closes. These tests prove `jobs.py`'s actual argv,
environment, stdin, persistence and settling rather than a mock standing in
for the claim that they work.

Two things do NOT mirror `test_agent_api.py`. First: a job's rows are written
through `get_session_factory()` (the app's real, engine-bound sessionmaker),
never the `session` fixture's rollback-wrapped connection -- a job must
outlive the one request that reads it back, and a connection whose every
write rolls back at test teardown cannot hold state across the several calls
these tests make, nor be seen by a second worker process. Second:
`AgentJob`/`AgentJobEvent` are `WorkspaceScoped`, so a job needs an
actually-COMMITTED workspace to satisfy its own foreign key -- `_provision`
below replicates `get_identity`'s own upsert chain directly against a real
session. Job and workspace rows this file commits are real; they are not
cleaned up, the same way `test_workspaces_api.py`'s own stable-uid fixtures
are not -- each test's own fresh `uuid4()` job id keeps them from colliding.

The in-process suite runs one worker; the deployment runs several.
`test_a_second_worker_process_watches_messages_stops_and_resumes_a_job` boots
a real second worker -- the same app under uvicorn, on the same database and
data directory -- and drives a job this process runs through it.
"""

